<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hawwara emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1580 CE – 1769 CE (approx.) · capital Farshut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Hawwara sheikhs, Arabised Berber lords who ruled most of Upper Egypt under Ottoman sovereignty until 1769.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a>, a tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> origin, were resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in the late 14th century. Under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ottoman-egypt">Ottoman Egypt</a> their sheikhs became the dominant power in the south, with their seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farshut">Farshut</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qena">Qena</a>, and from the later 16th century they acted as tax farmers and governors of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a> sheikhs controlled vast estates producing grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, collecting taxes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and keeping order among the tribes. Their court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farshut">Farshut</a> was known for hospitality and patronage. At its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammam-ibn-yusuf">Shaykh Hammam ibn Yusuf</a>, in the mid-18th century, their authority stretched over much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a> to Aswan.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammam-ibn-yusuf">Hammam</a> sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> rivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-bey-al-kabir">Ali Bey al-Kabir</a>. In 1769 Ali Bey's forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bey-abu-al-dhahab">Muhammad Bey Abu al-Dhahab</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a>, and Hammam died in flight, ending their autonomous power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1580 CE – 1769 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.14%;width:3.76%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1580 CE – 1769 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Farshut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hammam ibn Yusuf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Egypt</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hawwara of Upper Egypt, Shaykhdom of Hammam</dd></dl></aside></div>

